      ":question/id": "9922cdea-6f5c-5536-9c03-95fc4cb195b6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x^{2} + 2 x + 1)^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=x^{2} + 2 x + 1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2 x + 2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (2 x + 2) \\\\  &=2(x^{2} + 2 x + 1)(2 x + 2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=0} &=[2(x^{2} + 2 x + 1)(2 x + 2)]|_{x=0} \\\\  &=2((0)^{2} + 2 (0) + 1)(2 (0) + 2) \\\\  &=2(1)(2) \\\\  &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
