      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(5-3x)^{4},$ find the equation of the tangent at point $(2,1)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=5-3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  &=-12(5-3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} &=-12(5-3(2))^{3} \\\\  &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,1)$ is $m=12.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &=12(x-(2)) \\\\ y-(1) &=12 x - 24 \\\\ y &=12 x - 23\\end{aligned}\n$$",
