    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d834e652-be3b-54ff-a505-36615f2351ff",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(t^{2} + 3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(t^{2} + 3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=t^{2} + 3,\\,F(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g^{\u2032}(t) &=(t^{2} + 3)^{\u2032}=2 t, \\\\ F^{\u2032}(g(t)) = (g^{2})^{\u2032}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(t^{2} + 3)^{2}] &=h^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  &=F^{\u2032}(g(t))\\cdot g^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  &=2g\\cdot 2 t \\\\  &=4 t(t^{2} + 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
